Number = {7},
Pages = {490-501},
Abstract = {Currently, bicycle theft often goes unnoticed and is largely
   unchallenged, negatively impacting the use of this sustainable
   transportation mode. The present research brings attention to this issue
   by analyzing the multifaceted problem of bicycle theft in Montreal,
   Quebec, Canada. A bilingual online bicycle theft survey was designed for
   this purpose and answered by 2,039 Greater Montreal residents, yielding
   1,922 usable responses. This paper tries to understand bicycle theft by
   answering the questions who, what, where, how, and when. Findings from
   this study are useful to better understand and ultimately decrease
   bicycle theft in Montreal, but can also be beneficial for cyclists,
   police, and policy makers in other cities aiming to decrease bicycle
   theft.},
